      <DIV class=3DcontentMargins><SPAN class=3Dcontent>
      <TABLE width=3D504>
        <TBODY>
        <TR>
          <TD align=3Dleft></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
      <H1><A name=3Dxtocid0>White Paper</A></H1>
      <H1><A name=3Dxtocid1>High-Availability Solutions for</A></H1>
      <H1><B><A name=3Dxtocid2>SIP Enabled Voice-over-IP =
Networks</A></B></H1>
      <P><B>The rapid evolution of voice and data technology is =
significantly=20
      changing the business environment. The introduction of services =
such as=20
      instant messaging, integrated voice and email, and follow me =
services has=20
      contributed to a work environment where employees can communicate =
much=20
      more efficiently, thus increasing productivity. To meet the =
demands of the=20
      changing business environment businesses are beginning to deploy =
converged=20
      voice-and-data networks based on Session Initiation Protocol=20
      (SIP).</B><BR><BR></P>
      <P><B>SIP was originally defined in 1999, by the Internet =
Engineering Task=20
      Force (IETF) in RFC2543. The definition was the culmination of =
years of=20
      work in the IETF's MMUSIC Working Group to provide a mechanism to =
allow=20
      voice, video, and data to be integrated over the same network. SIP =

      provides the foundation for building converged networks that =
support=20
      seamless integration with traditional voice networks, email, the =
World=20
      Wide Web, and next-generation technologies such as instant =
messaging and=20
      3rd Generation Partnership Project (3GPP) mobile =
networks.</B><BR><BR></P>
      <P><B>As businesses continue to increase their use and reliance on =

      converged services, reliability and availability becomes =
increasingly=20
      important. This paper will introduce the key elements of a SIP =
network,=20
      further define the concept of high availability in SIP networks, =
and=20
      explore various techniques to increase the availability of =
SIP-based Voice=20
      over IP (VoIP) networks.</B><BR><BR></P>
      <P>Introduction to SIP Networks</P>
      <P>A SIP-based network is made up of several components:</P>
      <UL>
        <LI><B>SIP User Agent</B>=97any network end-point that can =
originate or=20
        terminate a SIP session. This might include a SIP-enabled =
telephone, a=20
        SIP PC-client (known as a "softphone"), or a SIP-enabled=20
gateway.<BR><BR>
        <LI><B>SIP Proxy Server</B>=97a call-control device that =
provides many=20
        services such as routing of SIP messages between SIP user =
agents<BR><BR>
        <LI><B>SIP Redirect Server</B>=97a call-control device that =
provides=20
        routing information to user agents when requested, giving the =
user agent=20
        an alternate uniform resource identifier (URI) or destination =
user agent=20
        server (UAS). <BR><BR>
        <LI><B>SIP Registrar Server</B>=97a device that stores the =
logical=20
        location of user agents within that domain or sub-domain. A SIP=20
        registrar server stores the location of user agents and =
dynamically=20
        updates its data via REGISTER messages.<BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <P>High Availability</P>
      <P>High-availability solutions for VoIP networks address the need =
for=20
      users to be able to place and receive calls under peak-load call =
rates or=20
      during device maintenance or failure. In addition to lost =
productivity,=20
      voice-network downtime often results in lost revenue, customer=20
      dissatisfaction, and even a weakened market position. Various =
situations=20
      can take devices off line, ranging from planned downtime for =
maintenance=20
      to catastrophic failure.</P>
      <P>High availability is not a specific technology, but a goal that =
is=20
      based on specific business needs. The complexity of a =
high-availability=20
      solution is determined by a company's availability needs and by =
the amount=20
      of system interruptions that can be tolerated by a business.=20
      High-availability solutions improve your VoIP network's =
availability,=20
      lower the costs associated with downtime by preventing outages, =
and reduce=20
      the impact of outages when they do occur. There are two key =
elements that=20
      contribute to availability in a VoIP network: capacity and =
redundancy.=20
      These concepts will now be explored further.</P>
      <P>Capacity</P>
      <P>Capacity is a measurement of the volume of traffic a network is =

      engineered to handle. Voice networks are typically engineered to =
handle a=20
      target peak-load capacity, commonly measured in calls per second. =
Target=20
      peak-load capacities are specific to each business and industry, =
and are=20
      based on measured busy-hour call rates. For example, the traffic =
during=20
      the busiest hour on Mother's Day may be the target peak-load =
capacity for=20
      a residential voice network.</P>
      <P>Redundancy</P>
      <P>Redundancy measures the extra capacity, to be used only in the =
event of=20
      an equipment failure, that is placed in a network. When a primary =
node in=20
      a voice network is taken down for maintenance or failure, a =
redundant=20
      secondary device can take over the processing of the voice =
traffic.</P>
      <P>Measuring Capacity and Redundancy</P>
      <P>Engineers commonly use the notation n+k to describe the =
engineered=20
      capacity (n) and redundancy (k) of nodes in an availability =
solution.=20
      Consider a network which distributes across three SIP proxies, =
each rated=20
      with an individual capacity of 100 calls per second. Assuming that =
the=20
      proxies could act together in various logical combinations, there =
are=20
      several options for engineering capacity (n) and redundancy =
(k).</P>
      <P>For example, each proxy could be deployed in a stand-alone=20
      configuration resulting in a total capacity (n =3D 3 proxy nodes) =
of 300=20
      calls; however, there would be no redundancy (k=3D0) since if one =
proxy=20
      fails there has been no arrangement for one of the other proxies =
to pick=20
      up the traffic.</P>
      <P>Since redundancy is critical it is more likely that the cluster =
will be=20
      engineered in a n+ k arrangement of (2 + 1) for a capacity of 200 =
calls=20
      per second. In this scenario, supporting the offered load of 200 =
calls per=20
      second only requires 2 out of the 3 proxies to process the call. =
The third=20
      proxy is available to take up the traffic (100 calls per second) =
in case=20
      one of the 2 primary proxies fails.</P>
      <P>A third scenario would be to engineer the cluster of three =
proxies to=20
      support an offer load of 100 calls per second. This scenario only =
requires=20
      one proxy to handle the offered load of 100 calls per second so =
the=20
      additional two proxies are providing redundant capacity in case of =
a=20
      failure. This would be referred to as a engineered capacity and =
redundancy=20
      (n + k) of (1 + 2).</P>
      <P>When engineering a high-availability solution for a voice =
network, one=20
      should determine the capacity (n) requirements first, and then add =

      additional redundant nodes (k) to achieve the desired =
availability.</P>
      <P>Designing a Highly Available Voice Network</P>
      <P>Following is an example and depiction (Figure 1) of a highly =
available=20
      voice network that can handle two outbound calls per second.</P>
      <P>In this example, each SIP gateway has four T1 trunk interfaces. =
At 24=20
      channels per T1, a single SIP gateway can handle 96 concurrent =
calls.=20
      Assuming an average call length of three minutes, 96 calls over =
180=20
      seconds is ~0.5 calls per second. The SIP proxy servers are each =
rated at=20
      100 calls per second.</P>
      <DL>
        <DD>Capacity of SIP gateway =3D 0.5 calls per second<BR><BR>
        <DD>Capacity of SIP Proxy Server =3D 100 calls per =
second<BR><BR>
        <DD>To handle the target capacity of two outbound calls per =
second, the=20
        network will need four gateways.<BR><BR></DD></DL><BR><B>Figure =
1</B>=20
      <BR><B>Voice Network Rated at Two Outbound Calls Per Second=20
      Capacity<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sipa=
v_wp/sipav_w0.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B>
      <P>Figure 2 shows the addition of one redundant device at each =
point along=20
      the call path, enabling the voice network to handle two outbound =
calls per=20
      second, with 25 percent redundant capacity. It should be noted =
that the=20
      overall additional capacity of this particular scenario is limited =
to 25%=20
      because only one additional gateway was added. The addition of =
each proxy=20
      actually doubles the capacity of each proxy cluster. Thus, the =
overall=20
      capacity of this scenario could be increase to 50% by simply =
adding one=20
      more gateway for a total of six gateways.</P><BR><B>Figure 2</B>=20
      <BR><B>Voice Network Rated at Two Outbound Calls Per Second with =
25=20
      Percent Redundant Gateway Capacity<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sipa=
v_wp/sipav_w1.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B>
      <P>Placing Intelligence in the Network</P>
      <P>There are several mechanisms that can be used to achieve load=20
      distribution and failover. These can be divided into two =
groups:</P>
      <DIV style=3D"MARGIN-LEFT: 2em">
      <P>1. placing intelligence in the previous hop</P>
      <P>2. placing intelligence in the redundant hop</P></DIV>
      <P>Intelligence in the Previous Hop</P>
      <P>Depending on the device in the previous hop, intelligence in =
the=20
      previous hop is typically provided through a backup proxy or DNS =
SRV (the=20
      concept of DNS SRV is defined later in this paper) in a user =
agent,=20
      through static routes or DNS SRV in a SIP proxy, and through dial =
peers or=20
      DNS SRV in a SIP gateway.</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid3>Backup Proxy, Static Routes, and Dial =
Peers</A></H3>
      <UL>
        <LI><EM>SIP user agents</EM> can typically set a backup proxy to =
take=20
        over if the primary proxy fails to respond. <BR><BR>
        <LI>Each static route in a <EM>SIP proxy server</EM> can =
typically be=20
        assigned a weight (for load balancing) and/or a priority (for =
redundancy=20
        failover). <BR><BR>
        <LI>Redundant dial peers can commonly be set up in <EM>SIP=20
        gateways</EM>, should the primary dial peer fail to respond. =
<BR><BR>
        <LI>Static routes can be configured for load balancing and =
redundancy,=20
        whereas a user agent's backup proxy and a SIP gateway's dial =
peers only=20
        provide redundancy failover.<BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid4>DNS SRV</A></H3>
      <P>Most network administrators are familiar with DNS A records. =
These=20
      records associate the name of a particular <EM>device</EM> with =
the IP=20
      addresses of the network cards of that device. DNS SRV records =
associate=20
      the name of a <EM>service</EM> with the IP addresses of devices =
which=20
      provide that service (SIP, for example). DNS SRV records are =
commonly=20
      configured to point to the SIP proxies in the local domain which =
accept=20
      SIP packets (over UDP): "_sip._udp.company.com". The specification =
for DNS=20
      SRV can be found in RFC 2782, at: <A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc2782.txt">http://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc2=
782.txt</A>=20
      .</P>
      <P>Each device listed in a DNS SRV record has an associated weight =
and=20
      priority. By adjusting the weight and priority of routes, system=20
      administrators can tailor the capacity and redundancy behavior of =
the SIP=20
      proxy server to their routing needs.</P>
      <P>To take advantage of DNS SRV records, SIP clients must be =
programmed=20
      with the intelligence to parse and act upon DNS SRV records. =
Although most=20
      client vendors claim to support DNS, they typically mean DNS A =
records,=20
      not DNS SRV records. It is important to verify that a SIP client=20
      specifically supports DNS SRV records.</P>
      <P>Intelligence in the Redundant Hop</P>
      <P>Intelligence can be placed in the redundant hop by adding a =
pair of=20
      redundant load balancers or enabling Virtual Router Redundancy =
Protocol=20
      intelligence in the redundant-hop devices.</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid5>Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol</A></H3>
      <P>Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP) defines a standard =
mechanism=20
      that enables a pair of redundant (1+1) servers on a LAN to =
negotiate=20
      ownership of a virtual IP address. One device is elected to be =
active and=20
      the other to be standby. If the active fails, the backup server =
takes=20
      over. An advantage of this scheme is that it achieves 1+1 =
redundancy=20
      without requiring any special intelligence in previous-hop SIP =
devices.=20
      However, this scheme only works for n=3D1 capacity and k=3D1 =
redundancy; it=20
      will not scale above 1+1. VRRP is described in RFC 2338, at: <A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc2338.txt">http://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc2=
338.txt</A>=20
      . Another protocol that accomplishes the same thing is Hot-Standby =
Routing=20
      Protocol (HSRP).</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid6>Load Balancers</A></H3>
      <P>Load balancers can be used to achieve redundancy and active =
load=20
      balancing. Load balancers can be configured as 1+1 redundant, =
using VRRP=20
      to negotiate active ownership of the virtual IP address of the =
redundant=20
      node. Load balancers are typically used when the redundant devices =
do not=20
      natively support VRRP themselves.</P>
      <P>Load Distribution and Failover</P>
      <P>The intelligence to distribute load and failover must be placed =
in=20
      devices in the network. When a call is placed through a highly =
available=20
      node in the network, the load balancing and redundancy =
intelligence can be=20
      placed either at the previous hop in or the redundant hop.</P>
      <P>Between a SIP user agent and its outbound SIP proxies, DNS SRV =
or=20
      backup proxy intelligence can be placed in the calling user agent =
or a=20
      pair of redundant load balancers or VRRP daemon (VRRPD) =
intelligence can=20
      be placed in the SIP proxies (Figure 3). With low call rates =
(under 100=20
      calls per second), placing VRRPD intelligence in the SIP proxies =
is=20
      preferable, due to the low implementation costs and the =
flexibility to=20
      serve less intelligent endpoints.</P><BR><B>Figure 3</B>=20
      <BR><B>Illustration of Two Options for Location of Load Failure =
and=20
      Distribution Intelligence Between SIP User Agents and =
Proxies<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sipa=
v_wp/sipav_w2.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B>
      <P>Between proxies, static routes or DNS SRV intelligence can be =
placed in=20
      the previous-hop SIP proxies or a pair of redundant load balancers =
or=20
      VRRPD intelligence can be added to the redundant SIP proxies =
(Figure 4).=20
      Using static routes is typically easiest.</P><BR><B>Figure 4</B>=20
      <BR><B>Illustration of Two Options for Location of Load Failure =
and=20
      Distribution Intelligence Between Interconnecting SIP Proxy=20
      Clusters<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sipa=
v_wp/sipav_w6.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B>
      <P>Between SIP proxies and outbound SIP gateways, static routes or =
DNS SRV=20
      intelligence can be used at the previous-hop SIP proxies or a pair =
of=20
      redundant load balancers or VRRPD intelligence can be placed at =
the SIP=20
      gateways (Figure 5). Using static routes in the previous-hop SIP =
proxies=20
      is typically easiest.</P><BR><B>Figure 5</B> <BR><B>Illustration =
of Two=20
      Options for Location of Load Failure and Distribution Intelligence =
from=20
      SIP Proxies to SIP Gateways<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sipa=
v_wp/sipav_w7.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B>
      <P>For inbound calls from SIP gateways to SIP proxies, either dial =
peers=20
      or DNS SRV intelligence can be used in the SIP gateways or a pair =
of=20
      redundant load balancers or VRRPD intelligence can be used in the =
SIP=20
      proxies (Figure 6). Dial peers are usually easiest, but may be =
unnecessary=20
      if the SIP proxies are already configured with load balancers or =
VRRPD=20
      intelligence.</P><BR><B>Figure 6</B> <BR><B>Illustration of Two =
Options=20
      for Location of Load Failure and Distribution Intelligence from =
SIP=20
      Gateways to SIP Proxies<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sipa=
v_wp/sipav_w8.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B>
      <P>Priority and Weight</P>
      <P>In redundancy schemes, routes to a primary and secondary server =
are=20
      negotiated based on their designated <EM>priority.</EM> A system=20
      administrator typically configures the priority of each route. For =
the=20
      solutions described in this document, the smaller number is =
considered to=20
      have the higher priority. For example, a route with a priority of =
2 is=20
      secondary to a route with a priority of 1. When two or more routes =
have=20
      the same priority, traffic is load balanced across both =
servers.</P>
      <P>In load-balancing schemes, new requests are distributed across=20
      available servers using a selection algorithm. A common selection=20
      algorithm for statistical load balancing is <EM>round-robin.</EM> =
A more=20
      general selection algorithm is <EM>weighted random,</EM> which =
distributes=20
      requests proportional to the <EM>weight</EM> assigned to each =
route. To=20
      load balance between routes to devices, the priority of the routes =
must be=20
      the same. A higher weight represents a proportionally higher =
device=20
      capacity. In many cases, the capacity of each device is the same, =
so the=20
      weight associated with the route to each device is 1. If the =
capacities of=20
      the devices are different, the weights are adjusted accordingly. =
For=20
      example, if device A has three-quarters the capacity of device B, =
then the=20
      weight of the route to device A would be 3, and the weight of the =
route to=20
      device B would be 4.</P>
      <P>By configuring the priority and weight of static route or DNS =
SRV=20
      entries, system administrators can tailor the capacity and =
redundancy=20
      behavior of their voice networks.</P>
      <P>Designing Voice Networks for 99.999 Percent or "Five Nines"=20
      Reliability</P>
      <P>With the knowledge of component-specific call success rates, it =
is=20
      possible to calculate aggregate call success rates. In the =
following=20
      examples, the impact of adding a redundant SIP proxy to a SIP =
proxy server=20
      farm is illustrated. In these examples, an individual SIP proxy =
call=20
      success rate of 99.96 percent is assumed.</P>
      <P>Figure 7 illustrates a single SIP proxy in the SIP farm=20
      (1+0).</P><BR><B>Figure 7</B> <BR><B>Call Success Rate without=20
      Redundancy<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sipa=
v_wp/sipav_w3.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B>
      <P>Improve the call success rate through the SIP proxy farm by =
adding a=20
      redundant SIP proxy server. Figure 8 illustrates a 1+1 redundancy=20
      configuration of two SIP proxy servers.</P><BR><B>Figure 8</B> =
<BR><B>Call=20
      Success Rate with One Redundant SIP Proxy Server<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sipa=
v_wp/sipav_w4.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B>
      <P>Putting It All Together</P>
      <P>By placing the desired combination of high-availability routing =

      intelligence in the network, we can complete the high-availability =

      solution. In the following example, the endpoints and gateways =
remain=20
      "simple," placing the high-availability intelligence in the SIP =
proxies=20
      (Figure 9).</P><BR><B>Figure 9</B> <BR><B>Highly Available =
SIP-based=20
      Network with Intelligence Placed in SIP Proxy Servers<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sipa=
v_wp/sipav_w5.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B>
      <P>Conclusion</P>
      <P>There are various availability solutions that are customizable =
to your=20
      business needs. Implementing a high-availability solution for your =
VoIP=20
      network will improve your voice network's uptime, and by =
preventing=20
      outages, will lower the costs associated with downtime.</P><BR>
      <P><BR></P></SPAN></DIV><!--googleoff: snippet-->
